00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:20.810
INDIAN CULTURES FELT SPIRITUALLY LINKED TO ANIMALS FOR PROVIDING THEM WITH LIFE'S

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:23.470
BEAR NECESSITIES, FOOD, CLOTHING.

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:29.970
PACIFIC NORTHWEST INDIANS MADE TOE

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.140
to appease the animal spirits.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:37.470
The hierarchy of animals were venerated by the native peoples

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:40.310
for behavioral traits the tribe admired.

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:46.730
Salmon, their main source of protein,

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:50.410
was treated with the reverence befitting a creature they believed

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:54.150
offered itself up willingly to ensure the tribe's survival.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.260
But there are no totems to any of the microbes.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.120
And yet, for every culture that has ever existed or ever will,

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.140
it is the microbes' astonishing abilities to chemically transform the world

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:12.680
that make life possible for all other forms of life.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:43.080
Beautiful, isn't it? Crystal clear water teeming with trout.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.980
At least that's what they tell me. No bites yet.

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:53.070
But the pristine lake you see now was a reeking quagmire of sulfurous mud and woody debris in the spring of 1980.

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:57.430
And before that, well, it didn't even exist.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.660
Mount St. Helens erupted with a magnitude 5.1 earthquake that released the largest landslide

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:15.890
in recorded history, resulting mudflows dammed up this river valley.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:24.490
As the dust settled, there was a lake here where there hadn't been before.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:29.640
But it was also a complex community of microbes.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.080
that helped rehabilitate Coldwater Lake here.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:35.640
It was the microbes that processed the sudden infusion

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.620
of nutrients and metals and caused suspended debris

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:41.340
in the water to precipitate and settle to the bottom,

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:43.580
thus paving the way for an ecosystem

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:46.120
that'll support stocked rainbow trout.

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:50.250
And so it goes the world over.

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:54.850
Microbes transform and regulate ecosystems large and small.

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:57.570
An ecosystem is a physical environment

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.930
along with all of the organisms that live.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.030
there. Over the next half hour, you'll see a variety of these environments which depend

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:13.790
upon microbial cycling of nitrogen, carbon, sulfur, and other essential mineral nutrients

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:23.370
long before there were animals or plants or fish or insects. When dinosaurs were still

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:29.960
a billion years in the future, microbes transformed the Earth's atmosphere from

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.780
from an unbreathable mixture of noxious gases to the oxygen-based air used by all the life

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:37.100
forms that were to follow.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:43.860
Today, microbes in the ocean affect the temperature of the planet.

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:50.470
Ocean algae produce a sulfur compound, which escapes from the ocean into the atmosphere.

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:56.990
In the atmosphere, the sulfur particles cause moisture in the air to form cloud droplets.

00:04:57.650 --> 00:04:59.970
With many droplets, we see a very...

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.560
very white cloud. With just a few, it looks dark. The degree of whiteness affects how

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:13.700
much of the sun's light and heat are reflected from Earth back into space, one of the things

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:21.300
that controls the Earth's temperature. Ongoing cycles such as this sustain life on our planet,

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:29.770
an ecosystem sometimes referred to as biosphere 1. But what might happen if humans attempted

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:29.970
to do.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:37.740
recreate this success in a custom-built, enclosed environment. In Arizona in the

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:49.660
mid-1980s, scientists built a small version of Earth. A tiny ocean, streams,

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:54.750
grasslands, even a desert, sealed by glass and steel.

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:12.380
Eight men and women were to live here, physically isolated from the outside, for two years.

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:17.700
Pyosphere 2 was a complete world.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:27.270
There were many species of plants and animals, seven ecological zones, recipe for life.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:34.720
Their goal was to create a self-sustaining living environment.

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:43.210
If successful, they would prove the possibility of sustaining life on other planets, such as Mars.

00:06:51.670 --> 00:06:54.850
In August of 1991, it was all ready.

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:59.970
The eight biospherians were about to breathe their last air from the outside world.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:18.950
The concept for the Biosphere Life Support System was straightforward, total recycling.

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:26.850
Everything needed for life, food, oxygen, materials of all kinds, would be used and reused.

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:29.790
Waste would be recovered and recycled.

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:36.600
Biosphere 2 would be completely self-contained, with nothing needed from the outside world.

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:45.830
The concept is sound, as demonstrated by this unique airtight globe called an ecosphere.

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:55.830
Inside, water, shrimp, algae, and a mix of aquatic bacteria can survive and thrive for years.

00:07:56.950 --> 00:07:59.430
Nutrients are used and reused.

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:03.560
Waste from one species is what another needs.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:09.760
In an endless cycle, all the nutrients for all the creatures are provided for.

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:15.630
Biosphere 2 set out to do the same thing on a larger scale.

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:19.720
For months, all was well.

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:23.590
The food consumed was grown within the biosphere.

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:29.430
The biospherians went about their work.

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:49.340
All ecological zones flourished, but unexpectedly oxygen levels began to fall.

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:52.840
One species after another died.

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:58.360
Birds disappeared, then the frogs.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.000
found it increasingly hard to breathe.

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:09.750
Urgent efforts at a remedy failed.

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:14.360
And, in desperation, oxygen was pumped in from outside.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:21.600
The sacred seal was broken.

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:28.960
Work inside continued, but the major feature of the grand experiment was gone.

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:37.280
Many explanations were proposed for the dramatic loss of oxygen.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:41.180
Plant growth in Biosphere 2 was less abundant than normal,

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:45.360
so oxygen from photosynthesis was less than expected.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.860
But this explained only a fraction of the loss.

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:55.720
The suspicion arose that a major part of the loss was due to the tiniest creatures,

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:59.590
that Biosphere 2 had failed in large part.

00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:10.160
because of microbes. The question is, why? What went wrong? We know that the oxygen levels

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:16.720
dropped by almost a third in only a few months, causing the extinction of many species within

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:28.400
Biosphere 2. Yet a conscious effort had been made to introduce plant, animal, and insect

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:29.960
species that would...

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:41.860
successfully form a food web, a web of life. But microbial species were added almost inadvertently.

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:51.420
Most of them, like Earth's, virtually unknown. The microbes did what they're supposed to do.

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:55.310
We created an imbalance by overfeeding them.

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:07.820
Other factors probably came into play, but there is a strong suspicion that in significant

00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:15.980
part one of humankind's most ambitious experiments was brought down in effect by a compost heap.

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:22.980
Fertilization of the soil created a microbial feeding frenzy.

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:29.170
Reproduction increased dramatically.

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:41.680
billions of trillions. This impacted the oxygen cycle. One microbe hardly makes a difference,

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:47.960
but trillions of rapidly reproducing bacteria consumed a huge amount of the oxygen

00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:56.200
within the enclosed ecosystem. Was Biosphere 2 a failure?

00:12:00.280 --> 00:12:02.030
Yes and no.

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:11.660
Biosphere 2 gave us an opportunity to begin developing ideas that we need to understand how our own biosphere works in detail.

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:18.030
We afford not to understand what microorganisms are and what they do.

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:21.840
Up till now we've gotten by without understanding them.

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:29.260
But we take for granted that Earth's biosphere will continue to hum along into the future without...

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:36.880
the sorts of significant problems that cause almost devastating trouble for Biosphere 2.

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:43.300
And we permit them to remain a mystery.

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:50.900
Before we begin predicting how our own biosphere will respond to human influences,

00:12:50.900 --> 00:12:53.960
we have to find out what these organisms do.

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:58.860
Without the microorganisms, there could be no plant life, there could be no animal life.

00:12:59.080 --> 00:12:59.980
We would...

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:22.280
disappear. Life is manifest all along America's eastern seaboard. Thousands of miles of coastline

00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:29.690
along the Atlantic and Gulf Coast are shallow marshes and swamps, an incredibly productive nursery.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:45.460
sea life, insects, microorganisms, this would be little more than a sterile sea of grass.

